WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 15: The Red River Bridge War: When Oklahoma and Texas Drew Guns

1
00:00:02.560 --> 00:00:05.200
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:06.480
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:11.280
[Rebecca] In the heat of the Great Depression, economic hardship pushed Americans to the brink.

4
00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:20.320
[Rebecca] Nowhere was that friction more colorful, bizarre, and explosive than on the border between Oklahoma and Texas in the summer of 1931.

5
00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:35.120
[Rebecca] When a private toll bridge company teamed up with a federal court to lock the gates on a brand new, taxpayer-funded free highway bridge, the eccentric pistol packing governor of Oklahoma, William Alfalfa Bill Murray decided that legal injunctions meant nothing.

6
00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:44.320
[Rebecca] He declared martial law, mobilized the National Guard with bayonets fixed, and marched to the Red River to face off against the legendary Texas Rangers.

7
00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:47.600
[Rebecca] Here is George with the story.

8
00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:51.440
[Rebecca] In the late 1920s, motorists traveling along U.S.

9
00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:59.760
[George] Highway 75 between Durrant, Oklahoma, and Denison, Texas faced an infuriating economic bottleneck at the Red River.

10
00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:08.160
[George] All vehicular traffic was forced to cross upon a rickety, privately owned wooden toll bridge operated by the Red River Bridge Company.

11
00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:22.320
[George] The private corporation charged an exorbitant toll of 75 cents per vehicle, equivalent to nearly $15 today, an immense financial burden for impoverished farmers and traveling workers during the depths of the Great Depression.

12
00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:41.040
[George] Responding to intense public outrage on both sides of the river, the state legislatures of Oklahoma and Texas agreed in 1929 to jointly fund and construct a modern, four-lane concrete highway bridge just a few hundred yards downstream, which would be entirely free to the traveling public.

13
00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:50.000
[George] By July of 1931, the beautiful new concrete bridge was finished, painted, and scheduled for a grand ribbon cutting ceremony.

14
00:01:50.000 --> 00:02:01.880
[George] Terrified that their immensely lucrative monopoly would be wiped out overnight, the Red River Bridge Company hired high-priced corporate attorneys, rushing to the Federal District Court in Houston, Texas.

15
00:02:02.120 --> 00:02:08.600
[George] On July 10, just days before the scheduled opening, Federal District Judge Joseph C.

16
00:02:08.840 --> 00:02:21.320
[George] Hutcheson issued an 11th hour injunction, prohibiting both states from opening the free bridge to public traffic until the company's compensation claims of nearly $200,000 were resolved in court.

17
00:02:21.320 --> 00:02:33.960
[George] The toll bridge company had operated without competition since 1923, extracting tolls from thousands of desperate migrant farm families and oilfield rougnecks who had no other route across the river.

18
00:02:33.960 --> 00:02:46.280
[George] Local chambers of commerce in Durant and Denison had pleaded with both state governments for years to replace the private toll monopoly, arguing that free interstate commerce was the lifeblood of regional recovery.

19
00:02:46.280 --> 00:02:57.480
[George] In Austin, Texas Governor Ross Sterling was an establishment oil executive and former founder of the Humble Oil Company who believed in the strict supremacy of federal court rulings.

20
00:02:57.480 --> 00:03:03.880
[George] When the Houston Federal injunction arrived on his desk, Governor Sterling complied without hesitation.

21
00:03:03.880 --> 00:03:13.480
[George] He ordered the Texas Highway Commission to place massive timber barricades, heavy chains, and iron padlocks across the southern approach to the free concrete bridge.

22
00:03:13.480 --> 00:03:28.760
[George] To enforce the closure, Sterling dispatched three heavily armed, stone-faced Texas Rangers, who stood guard at the locked barrier with holstered revolvers, forcing all southbound motorists to turn back and pay the toll to the private bridge company.

23
00:03:28.760 --> 00:03:35.960
[George] Up in Oklahoma City, however, the newly inaugurated governor of Oklahoma was a very different kind of politician.

24
00:03:35.960 --> 00:03:42.680
[George] His name was William Henry Davis Murray, universally known to friends and foes alike as Alfalfa Bill.

25
00:03:42.680 --> 00:03:47.280
[George] Alfalfa Bill was an iconic, larger-than-life frontier populist.

26
00:03:44.360 --> 00:04:03.360
[George] He wore rumpled linen suits stained with tobacco juice, chewed plug tobacco incessantly, lived in a sod-chinked log cabin, and possessed an explosive temper combined with an absolute hatred for federal judges, corporate monopolies, and the state of Texas.

27
00:04:03.360 --> 00:04:14.720
[George] Murray had previously declared martial law over the state's oil fields to force oil companies to halt production and raise crude prices, proving to Texans that he was not an executive who bluffed.

28
00:04:14.720 --> 00:04:24.400
[George] Murray's unyielding defiance reflected the deep frustration of Western farmers who felt abandoned by the federal government and betrayed by Eastern banking interests.

29
00:04:24.400 --> 00:04:31.360
[George] When Governor Murray received word that Texas had padlocked the public bridge, he flew into a towering rage.

30
00:04:31.360 --> 00:04:38.960
[George] Murray was a skilled constitutional scholar who had served as the president of Oklahoma's 1906 Constitutional Convention.

31
00:04:38.960 --> 00:04:54.320
[George] He asserted that under the historic 1819 Adams Honus Treaty between the United States and the Kingdom of Spain, the international boundary, and subsequently the state line between Oklahoma and Texas, was established at the south bank of the Red River.

32
00:04:54.320 --> 00:04:56.640
[George] Not the center of the river channel.

33
00:04:56.640 --> 00:05:06.880
[George] Therefore, Murray argued, both approaches to the bridge, including the Southern Gate on the Texas shoreline, were legally situated within the sovereign territory of Oklahoma.

34
00:05:06.880 --> 00:05:18.080
[George] Murray publicly announced that a federal judge sitting in Houston had zero constitutional authority over Oklahoma state highways, declaring, our state sovereignty is at stake.

35
00:05:18.080 --> 00:05:24.160
[George] And I will not permit a federal court to assist a thieving corporation in fleecing the citizens of Oklahoma.

36
00:05:24.160 --> 00:05:38.680
[George] On July 16, Governor Murray issued an official executive decree commanding Oklahoma Highway crews to drive bulldozers across the bridge, tear down the Texas barricades with crowbars, and open the road to public traffic.

37
00:05:38.680 --> 00:05:45.640
[George] Texas road crews promptly rebuilt the barricades, and the Texas Rangers stood watch with hands on their gun belts.

38
00:05:45.640 --> 00:05:56.040
[George] The Oklahoma Highway Commission sent fleets of heavy state steam tractors and dump trucks to support the road crews, creating an armed perimeter along the Northern River bluff.

39
00:05:56.040 --> 00:05:59.160
[George] Alfalfa Bill was not a man to be trifled with.

40
00:05:59.160 --> 00:06:12.120
[George] On July 24, 1931, Governor Murray invoked his executive war powers, declared martial law over the entire Red River Bridge crossing, and mobilized the Oklahoma National Guard.

41
00:06:12.120 --> 00:06:18.440
[George] He dispatched a company of 35 armed guardsmen under the command of Adjutant General Charles Barrett.

42
00:06:18.440 --> 00:06:33.960
[George] Equipped with Springfield rifles, fixed bayonets and light machine guns, the Oklahoma soldiers marched onto the bridge, established fortified sandbag positions along the South Shoreline, and set up a permanent military checkpoint on the Texas side.

43
00:06:33.960 --> 00:06:45.800
[George] Governor Murray personally arrived at the frontline encampment, wearing an oversized white linen suit, chewing tobacco, and conspicuously brandishing an antique revolver tucked into his belt.

44
00:06:45.800 --> 00:06:49.720
[George] A carnival atmosphere erupted along both banks of the river.

45
00:06:49.720 --> 00:07:03.080
[George] Over five thousand cheering spectators flocked to the scene, buying barbecue, eating cold watermelon, and waving flags while newspapers across the nation ran breathless headlines covering the Red River Bridge War.

46
00:07:03.080 --> 00:07:16.240
[George] In Austin, Governor Sterling placed the Texas National Guard on standby, and the entire nation watched with bated breath, wondering if two American states were actually about to engage in armed warfare.

47
00:07:16.560 --> 00:07:28.880
[George] Motorists camped along the roadside in the July heat, transforming the Texas Oklahoma border into an impromptu frontier festival with fiddlers, watermelon feasts, and political stump speeches.

48
00:07:28.880 --> 00:07:33.760
[George] Fortunately, common sense prevailed before a single shot could be discharged.

49
00:07:33.760 --> 00:07:45.360
[George] Recognizing the sheer absurdity of two American states mobilizing infantry against one another during an economic depression, the Texas state legislature convened an emergency session in Austin.

50
00:07:45.360 --> 00:07:54.480
[George] Lawmakers rushed through a special appropriation bill authorizing the state to settle the private bridge company's financial claims through binding arbitration.

51
00:07:54.480 --> 00:08:10.800
[George] Simultaneously, recognizing that public fury had reached a dangerous flashpoint, Federal Judge Hutcheson in Houston quietly dissolved the injunction, acknowledging that the public interest in an open interstate highway far outweighed the private profits of a toll corporation.

52
00:08:10.800 --> 00:08:17.680
[George] On August 6, 1931, the heavy chains and padlocks were removed from the gates permanently.

53
00:08:17.680 --> 00:08:29.360
[George] With cheering motorists blaring their horns in celebration, a parade of thousands of cars streamed across the free concrete bridge, linking Oklahoma and Texas without paying a single dime.

54
00:08:29.360 --> 00:08:45.040
[George] Governor Murray returned to the Oklahoma Capitol a triumphant working class folk hero, having demonstrated that a stubborn populist governor with a handful of bayonets could stare down corporate monopolies, federal courts, and the Texas Rangers, and win.

55
00:08:45.040 --> 00:08:56.320
[George] When the court decree was signed, Governor Murray personally cut the ceremonial red ribbon, waving his antique revolver to signal the first line of Oklahoma motorists across the span.

56
00:08:56.320 --> 00:09:02.200
[George] The Red River Bridge War elevated Alfalfa Bill Murray into a national populist icon.

57
00:09:02.200 --> 00:09:12.040
[George] At a time when millions of working-class Americans felt crushed by the unseen machinery of Wall Street banks, giant utility monopolies, and distant courts.

58
00:09:12.040 --> 00:09:20.120
[George] Murray's bold defiance on the banks of the Red River was celebrated as a glorious triumph for the common man against corporate exploitation.

59
00:09:20.120 --> 00:09:39.400
[George] In 1932, Time magazine put Murray's colorful, unkempt face on its cover, and the Oklahoma governor launched a spirited, nationwide campaign for the Democratic presidential nomination, riding across the country in coach train cars and preaching radical economic relief for the working poor.

60
00:09:39.400 --> 00:09:51.160
[George] The concrete bridge that Alfalfa Bill opened with bayonets served millions of travelers on Highway 75 for over 60 years, until it was replaced by a modern span in 1995.

61
00:09:51.160 --> 00:10:07.480
[George] Yet, decades later, drivers crossing the red waters of the river still glanced down at the mud banks, smiling at the memory of that sweltering summer when an Oklahoma governor pulled an antique pistol, aimed it across the river at Texas, and won a war for a free road.

62
00:10:07.480 --> 00:10:18.120
[George] To this day, local historians in Bryan County celebrate the annual anniversary of the bridge war, honoring the eccentric governor who defended a free highway.

63
00:10:18.520 --> 00:10:28.120
[Rebecca] The Red River Bridge War of 1931 is a colorful reminder of the fierce independence and frontier theatricality of American politics during the Great Depression.

64
00:10:28.120 --> 00:10:35.640
[Rebecca] It proves that sometimes, the line between legal standoff and high comedy is as thin as the muddy waters of the Red River.

65
00:10:35.640 --> 00:10:38.680
[Rebecca] Thank you for listening to most fascinating stories.

66
00:10:38.680 --> 00:10:39.880
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

67
00:10:39.880 --> 00:10:43.240
[Rebecca] Join us next time for another forgotten chapter of history.

68
00:10:43.240 --> 00:10:44.920
[Rebecca] Until then, stay curious.

